<!-- @before 2. Handling Server-Side Rate Limits (HTTP 429) :: guide -->
### A shorter retry budget cannot shorten the embargo

Retry-After can be delay-seconds or an HTTP-date. Preserve the affected bucket’s deadline; if it exceeds the client’s retry budget, surface or stop the attempt. Concurrent responses must not replace a longer active deadline with a shorter one. [429 semantics](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6585.html#section-4) and [Retry-After](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-10.2.3).

<!-- @before 3. UI Rate Limiting (Disabling Interactions) :: staff -->
### A disabled button is not deduplication

UI controls reduce accidental attempts within one experience. They do not cover process death, another device or an already in-flight command. Durable mutations still require their operation identity and backend replay contract.

<!-- @before 1. Client-Side Throttling (Coroutines & Flow) :: visual -->
### Three owners enforce three different rules

```mermaid
flowchart TB
 p["UI pacing Debounce or interaction policy"]
 q["Current query owner Reject stale completion"]
 s["Server admission Bucket and retry policy"]
 r["Repository workflow Cancellation and safe retry"]
 p -->|"candidate attempt"| r
 r -->|"request admission"| s
 s -->|"response or 429"| r
 r -->|"result with request identity"| q
```

Pacing reduces attempts, the screen validates current request identity, and the server enforces admission. No one of these substitutes for the other two.
